[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 803,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 607,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122883,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 607,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adrenal myelolipoma is a benign tumour made of mature adipose tissue and haematopoietic cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 804,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 608,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122884,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 608,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8870/0 Adrenal myelolipoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 805,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 609,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122885,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 609,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F37.Y &amp; XH17C5 Other specified benign neoplasm of endocrine glands &amp; Myelolipoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 806,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 610,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122886,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 610,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 807,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 611,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122887,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 611,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 808,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 612,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122888,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 612,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most adrenal myelolipomas occur in one gland, and bilaterality accounts for about 5% of cases {32275787}. Rare examples occur in adrenal rests {29164520}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 809,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 613,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122889,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 613,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myelolipoma often manifests with asymptomatic incidental adrenal lesions that may be detected as a result of nonspecific abdominal discomfort or pain {19481963}. Large myelolipomas, especially giant myelolipomas (&gt;&nbsp;100&nbsp;mm), can cause compression, rupture, or haemorrhage {25878407; 33514622}. They are hormonally inactive unless combined with a functioning adrenocortical tumour or occurring in the setting of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) {29164520; 28143811; 33471666}. In CAH, myelolipomas are more frequently bilateral {33471666}. Most are hypoechoic on ultrasound and are well-demarcated heterogeneous lesions on CT {33532014; 29194699}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 810,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 614,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122890,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 614,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adrenal myelolipomas account for 3.3&ndash;6.5% of all primary adrenal tumours, with no sex bias and an average age of 50&ndash;60&nbsp;years {29164520}. In patients with CAH, myelolipomas account for 25% of CAH-related adrenal tumours {33471666}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 811,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 615,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122891,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 615,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 812,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 616,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122892,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 616,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Several hypotheses have been suggested, including (1)&nbsp;interactions between two stem cell progenitors of adipocytes and bone marrow cells; (2)&nbsp;inflammation- or stress-induced metaplastic changes of blood capillary reticuloendothelial cells; (3)&nbsp;chromosomal translocation t(3;21)(q25;p11) or non-random X-chromosome inactivation {11996801; 16819325}; and (4)&nbsp;hormonal factors, mostly linked to ACTH overexpression or an androgen-mediated stimulus, as well as overexpression of MC2R and AR {29164520; 28143811; 33622747; 27307426; 24884994}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 819,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 623,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122893,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 623,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adrenal myelolipoma is a well-demarcated yellow or red nodule with a mean size of 100&nbsp;mm (range: 5&ndash;430&nbsp;mm) {29164520} and a median size of 23&nbsp;mm {32275787}. The weight of giant myelolipomas can exceed 11&nbsp;kg {12746567}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 813,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 617,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122894,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 617,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myelolipoma is composed of mature adipocytes and all three haematopoietic cell lineages. Calcifications and osseous metaplasia can occur. Myelolipoma can develop in combination with adrenocortical tumours, ganglioneuroma, hibernoma, bilateral macronodular adrenocortical disease, and CAH-related compact cell adrenocortical hyperplasia {29164520; 28143811; 28382483}. Myelolipomas with extensive haemorrhage can be mistaken for an adrenal haematoma or malignancy. The differential diagnosis includes adrenal lipoma, angiomyolipoma, and liposarcoma {29164520; 28143811}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 814,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 618,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122895,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 618,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Smears show scattered haematopoietic cells admixed with mature fat cells in a lipid-rich background, to suggest myelolipoma {18855887; 10572274; 32988789; 15376200}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 815,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 619,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122896,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 619,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 818,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 622,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122897,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 622,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a circumscribed adrenal tumour composed of mature adipocytes and haematopoietic cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 816,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 620,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122898,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 620,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 817,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 621,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 122899,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 128,
        "headingId": 621,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>No malignant progression has been reported. Acute haemorrhage and tumour rupture can (rarely) occur in large myelolipomas, usually those &gt;&nbsp;80&nbsp;mm. Therefore, surgery can be considered for large and/or symptomatic tumours, with complete recovery expected.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:37:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]